  "soft_conclusions": [
    "Repeating an identical view is not a discovery action anywhere: across 12 cells in 3 localities, repeats added 0 in-target IDs beyond the best single search, while ID sets were stable (mean pairwise Jaccard 0.974-0.988). Rank order is unstable everywhere (core IDs shift 2.4-38.9 positions).",
    "Split is the strongest discovery action in total in all three localities (London +440, Austin +41, Vashi +18 new IDs) and the only action with exclusive finds in most cells. Its cost is 4 pages per cell.",
    "Zoom-in value depends on how badly the integer zoom fit overshoots the cell: in London the fitted baseline zoom 16 views ~1.19 km for a 750 m cell, and one zoom-in page alone found 62-76 new IDs per cell; in Vashi and Austin (zoom 17, ~0.83-0.90 km view) zoom-in found 0-5 per cell. Check viewport-span vs cell-span before choosing.",
    "Shift is the weakest action overall (London 44, Austin 6, Vashi 5 new) and almost everything it finds, split also finds.",
    "The size of what one search misses scales with density: London observed IDs went 153 -> 622 (+307%), Austin 245 -> 288 (+18%), Vashi 87 -> 108 (+24%). A single view is worst exactly where places are densest.",
    "Raw rows never measure local density: London pages returned 117-120 raw rows with only 32-55 in-target; Vashi SW returned ~90 raw rows with 0 in-target under every action in both experiments.",
    "A cell can be truly empty for a keyword (Vashi SW: 0 in-target across 10 searches and 3 action types) - stop spending on it without a reference check."
  ],
  "claim": "Soft conclusions from three localities and one keyword. Observed unions are larger observed sets, not every place in any area. Totals remain unknown everywhere."
